  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 223 PM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge of high pressure will remain anchored over\nthe Central and Southern Plains this weekend, which will keep \nhot, mid summer conditions in place across the Tri-State Area. \nAs we move into next workweek, weather models show the ridge of \nhigh pressure gradually flattening and shifting westward toward \nthe Four Corners Region. This retrogression will shift our local\nupper level wind pattern to the west/northwest, allowing for \nperiodic disturbances to slide along the edge of the ridge and \nacross the High Plains. \n\nSeveral hot days are in store for the region starting Saturday.\nForecast confidence is very high with 25th to 75th percentile \nmax temperatures with only a 3-4 degree spread, with Saturday \nhigh temperatures ranging from the upper 90s to low 100s, and \nSunday-Monday ranging from the low to mid 100s for the entire \narea. Areas along and east of US Highway 83 could approach Heat \nAdvisory thresholds, with heat index values topping out around \nSunday and Monday. As the ridge retreats westward Tuesday and \nWednesday, temperatures will gradually ease with model variance \nbecoming wider (highs ranging from the low to mid 90s). \n\nA steady tap of moisture wrapping around the upper level high \npressure system will keep atmospheric moisture levels unusually \nhigh across the region between the 75th and 99th percentile \n(well above normal) for late July. Saturday into Sunday will be \nmainly diurnally driven with storms initiating off the higher \nterrain of the Front Range/Palmer Divide during the afternoon, \nthen drive eastward. Confidence is low that storms will actually\nmake it over to extreme eastern CO counties. Chances for \nprecipitation increase and spread further east as northwest flow\naloft sharpens and some instability in the upper flow occurs \n(shortwave troughs moving through). With high atmospheric \nmoisture, and a lack of mid level wind support leading to likely\nslow storm movement, heavy rain and perhaps some local minor \nflooding could occur under persistent / repeating storm cores. \nOtherwise, severe weather is not anticipated at this time.",
